            <ArticleTitle>Prevention of events with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibition (the PEACE study design). Prevention of Events with Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibition.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>25H-30H</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The Prevention of Events with Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibition (PEACE) trial is an 8,100 patient, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial designed to determine the usefulness of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors in treating coronary patients with preserved left ventricular ejection fraction. The hypothesis being tested in this trial is that patients with coronary disease and ejection fraction &gt; or =40% who are treated with ACE inhibitors will experience a reduction in the incidence of cardiovascular death, nonfatal myocardial infarction, or a revascularization procedure compared with patients treated with conventional therapy. The design of the PEACE trial is described herein.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
